00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.760 align:start position:0%
 
with the other souls of youtubers hyping  up code vain recently the  intersectionality of anime and Dark  Souls is upon us and there's no better  anime Souls game out right now than Momo  Dora reverie under the moonlight  Momo Dora is an indie Metroidvania title  that released in early 2016  it offers strategic combat a focus on  bosses and a healing checkpoint system  similar to that of the souls series  coupled with beautiful pixel art  graphics and a bit of anime Ness  to be fair definitely not as much as  code vein though reverie under the  moonlight is the fourth installment in  the momo Dora series but doesn't require  any knowledge or experience with the  previous games you play as Tahoe a leaf  wielding priestess who travels to the  kingdom of karst to end the source of a  darkness which is plaguing her land you  have two weapons a leaf which acts as  your sword and a bow this plus an  invincible role makes up the majority of  your combat tool similar to souls the  combat is responsive and satisfying it  has a consumable heal that were charges  at checkpoint markers like souls - in  this game  bells serve as bonfires restoring your  health and consumables saving your  progress and eventually allowing you to  warp between zones  however this mechanic isn't exactly a  one-to-one match as Souls Dark Souls  each death sends you back to a bonfire  without any of your souls of her  experience points but the world is  exactly as it is when you died if you  had opened up a shortcut right before  your death the shortcut would still be  open this can create some strategic  deaths in that if you sacrifice yourself  to open up a shortcut and die after  opening up the shortcut you will still  have made progress with that death but  in Momo Dora the bells act more like  traditional saves any progress you make  is voided by death if you don't ring a  bell opening a shortcut or defeating a  boss must be saved by ringing a bell if  you die before doing so you'll have to  redo the boss fight or reopen the  shortcut this takes away the ability of  a strategic death because any death will  reset your experience unless  you ring a bell so speaking of boss  fights they're one of the main focuses  of the game there are eight boss fights  in the game including one which is  repeated and an optional one there is  somewhat of a ninth but the fight is  kind of weird in the boss doesn't have a  health bar so I'm not including him in  the list but I think he counts as a boss  fight I don't know I did really enjoy  every boss fight in the game even the  repeated one each boss was stylistically  different in visuals and gameplay and  although some were a bit exploitable to  cheese they were all an engaging  challenge I felt each one was a  different fight each one was engaging  fun had their different mechanics even  the boss which had a hitbox of giant  boobs was a pretty fun fight that's the  anime side of the game showing overall  the difficulty was a bit lower than a  souls game but each fight ended with a  significant feeling of accomplishment  and achievement going through the game  the first time each boss did feel like a  significant challenge and subsequent  playthroughs allowed for more challenge  in trying to beat the balls without  getting hit as you get rewarded with a  special item for most of the bosses if  you beat them without taking any damage  so overall difficulty wise I'd say  there's a satisfying amount each boss  has its own zone and each zone matches  the thematic differences of the bosses  the game is only around 6 hours so  you're not spending too much time in  anyone's own but the time you do spend  allows you to really absorb the feeling  that was intended for each zone the  visuals and backgrounds are done  beautifully and really make the world  feel real and immersive I caught myself  disappointed in how much I was focusing  on the foreground and enemies because I  was missing out on some of the great  backgrounds which really absorbed me and  I would just sit in the stare for a  while at something kaho and the NPC's  were actually given a good amount of  expression even despite the fact that  there's semi basic sprites I was really  surprised by how much tone they often  expressed in their little animations and  facial expressions I totally got the  intended feeling when Kath give a bit of  an inquisitive and accusatory finger  point or whenever kaho gets a bit  confused at some other characters  questions the music and sound design  weren't quite as good as the visuals but  they were still pretty great the music  is generally pretty  with either no background music or like  tunes but it matches with the mood of  each zone really well soundtracks for  the bosses are well done and emotional  they definitely add to each foot and  while I didn't find any of them to be  horribly memorable I still really  enjoyed each one the sound effects often  match this nest Game Boy style that the  visuals harken back to a lot of the  attacks and explosions reminded me of  Gen 1 &amp; 2 Pokemon attack sound effects  that I used to ear on my Game Boy which  was a really cool trip of nostalgia for  the world design captures a great  interconnectivity and exploration focus  that adds to the immersion the visuals  and sound setup the world is a blast to  sift and search through and I found  myself really enthralled with searching  and trying to figure out where I was the  world map helps you keep some amount of  bearings concerning your location so you  don't get totally lost what really makes  this work is that the game opens up  after the tutorial area and allows you  to choose where you want to go for  essentially all of the mid-game you need  four pieces of a crest to access the  final two areas but the order in which  you get these pieces is completely up to  you this means that there's a ton of  variety in the next four zones because  you get to decide which order they're  played in and each crispies gives you a  significant ability upgrade in  Metroidvania fashion like being able to  warp between bells or dodge midair this  gives a real sense of progression you'll  also receive smaller D upgrades either  through purchasing items finding secrets  or being rewarded for being bosses  without taking damage like I said before  these upgrades include things like  poison damage on your arrows or  defensive spells these items and the  crest pieces really let the gameplay  progress so that fighting the final boss  feels radically different than fighting  the first one although you still can  beat the final boss using only the tools  from the beginning of the game which  again allows for a significant player  choice you can either try and max out  find every item and use every tool in  your kit to beat the later bosses or you  can keep it very vanilla and use only a  couple upgrades or none at all and stick  to the same fighting style the whole  game an additional point on this side of  progression unlike souls there isn't a  leveling system in this game monies  which are this game's experience points  are only used to purchase items and the  only way to increase damage output is  through items or weapon  grades which are again item based you're  never spending any time in Maxine if  it's better to increase your health or  defense or something like that because  those things are only affected by item  pickups like vitality upgrades which you  find scattered throughout the world on  the characters and story it maintains a  soul's approach of bare-bones  explanations short NPC interactions and  subtle hints throughout the game  there isn't much development in most of  the characters as they are generally  pretty static having fairly unchanging  dialogue and remaining in the same place  one character I mentioned before cath  get some screen time and even helps out  in one of the boss fights but other than  her there's not much going on character  wise there are a couple small character  progressions as you explore through the  game that do add to the world but it's  nothing significant and there's not a  lot of significant character development  which is okay as the game doesn't make  it a priority but having more of that is  always nice the story is briefly  explained of player and item  descriptions give more exposition about  the world it isn't anything super deep  or narrative ly enthralling but the  small character stories help build a  fairly dark and somber game which  juxtaposes interestingly with Cohoes  overall happier demeanor these story and  characters help get the job done to  allow the gameplay visuals and sound to  sustain a really great game although the  game is pretty short it's quite a ride  it's only ten bucks and I'd say it's  very worth it as I've already gone  through the game about three times and  have had a great time in each play  through the exploration world  connectivity and strategic combat are  all done well enough to the point that  I'd say almost any souls fan would quite  enjoy this game and everything else  comes together to make this a great game  in its own right so that about ends up  most of my opinions on mode or reverie  under the moonlight I really recommend  it and thank you for watching the video  see you in the next one

